<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Two departures from Android's defaults, both of them about the same person: a
  self-hoster whose vault runs on a machine they own. Lint objects to both
  (InsecureBaseConfiguration, AcceptsUserCertificates) and lint is right to ask;
  the answers are below, and the `tools:ignore` is the record that they were
  given rather than a warning swept up.

  cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
    A Vellum server on a home network is reached at http://192.168.1.x:5173 or
    http://vault.lan, because there is no certificate authority on earth that
    will issue for those names. Refusing cleartext would not make that setup
    safer; it would make the app useless to it, and push the owner into a
    browser with no gate at all. What keeps this narrow is not the transport
    policy — it is that the app talks to exactly ONE host, the one its owner
    typed and this app verified, and that a host reached over https can never be
    downgraded to http by a link (VellumPlugin.shouldOverrideLoad compares the
    scheme, not only the name).

  <certificates src="user" />
    The other half of the same story: a self-hoster with a private CA, or a
    Tailscale/step-ca certificate installed on their own phone, should have their
    own server trusted by their own app. Android stopped honouring the user store
    by default in API 24; for an app whose entire job is reaching one personal
    server, that default is aimed at the wrong threat.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <base-config
        cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" tools:ignore="AcceptsUserCertificates" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
